Linux is an open-source operating system based on Unix. It is widely used for servers, cloud platforms, DevOps tools, and production environments because it is stable, secure, scalable, and cost-effective.

============================================================
Linux Architecture
============================================================
Linux follows a layered architecture:

------------------------------------
Kernel
------------------------------------
Core of the OS
Manages CPU, memory, processes, devices, and file systems
------------------------------------
Shell
------------------------------------
Interface between user and kernel
Takes commands from user and executes them
Examples: bash, sh, zsh
------------------------------------
System Libraries
------------------------------------
Provide functions for applications to interact with the kernel
------------------------------------
Applications
------------------------------------
User programs like editors, browsers, servers, DevOps tools


============================================================
What is a Linux Distribution?
============================================================
A Linux distribution (distro) is a complete OS package containing:

Linux Kernel
Package manager
System tools
Applications

------------------------------------
Popular Linux Distributions:
------------------------------------
Ubuntu – beginner-friendly, widely used in cloud
CentOS / Rocky Linux – server environments
Red Hat (RHEL) – enterprise use
Amazon Linux – AWS optimized

============================================================
File System Structure 
============================================================
Linux follows a single root (/) directory:
| Directory | Purpose                |
| --------- | ---------------------- |
| `/`       | Root directory         |
| `/home`   | User home directories  |
| `/etc`    | Configuration files    |
| `/var`    | Logs and variable data |
| `/bin`    | Essential commands     |
| `/usr`    | User programs          |
| `/tmp`    | Temporary files        |
| `/opt`    | Optional software      |

============================================================
Basic Linux Commands
============================================================
------------------------------------
File & Directory Commands
------------------------------------
ls        # list files
pwd       # present working directory
cd        # change directory
mkdir     # create directory
rm        # delete file/directory
cp        # copy files
mv        # move/rename files

------------------------------------
File Viewing
------------------------------------
cat       # view file
less      # scroll view
head      # first lines
tail      # last lines

------------------------------------
Permissions
------------------------------------
chmod     # change permissions
chown     # change owner

Permission format:
r = read, w = write, x = execute

------------------------------------
User & Process Management
------------------------------------
whoami    # current user
id        # user info
ps        # running processes
top       # real-time processes
kill      # terminate process

------------------------------------
Package Management
------------------------------------
apt install nginx     # Ubuntu
yum install nginx     # CentOS
dnf install nginx     # RHEL






